- op: discuss  # bcca
  at: '2026-07-30T17:20:26Z'  # bcca
  by: agent  # bcca
  actor: jgstern_agent  # bcca
  clock: 6  # bcca
  nonce: bcca  # bcca
  message: "WONT_DO — the premise is FALSE, established by reading the lookup rather than by a bakeoff.\n\nFiled claim: 'DEFAULT_EDGE_TYPE_WEIGHTS covers 12/54 types; 14118 edges silently get 0.0 centrality weight.'\n\nThe coverage half re-measures (numbers moved because the edge-type registry shrank 54->25 in the endpoint_shape fold): the weights map covers 11 types, the self-corpus emits 17, so 8 emitted types have no entry, spanning 18241 of 138626 edges (13.2%) — references, instantiates, inherits, overrides, module_attr_ref, uses, decorated_by, defines_target. So the gap is real and slightly larger than filed.\n\nBut the CONSEQUENCE claim is wrong. ranking.py:335 is 'type_weight = edge_type_weights.get(edge.edge_type, 0.5)'. An unlisted type gets 0.5, never 0.0 — and 0.5 is not an arbitrary fallback, it is the same weight the map assigns structural 'implements' (named in the comment two lines above). With no weights map at all the default is 1.0. Nothing is silently zeroed, so no edge is silently excluded from centrality.\n\nRESIDUAL, stated honestly rather than folded into a close: whether 0.5 is the RIGHT weight for those 8 types — particularly 'references', which is high-volume — is a genuine ranking-TUNING question. It is not this item, which is filed as a silent-zero data-loss bug. Tuning centrality weights shifts every centrality score and therefore slice/compact/sketch output, so it needs a real-repo A/B rather than a unit test (the WI-batit lesson: a unit test can pass while the live behaviour is unmoved). Anyone picking that up should file it as a tuning item with a before/after, not reopen this one."  # bcca
